Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Against the Current, Bar 17
- Story & Context: During an early recording pass, Mara notices that one kora phrase appears to sit behind the ferry engine pulse for sixteen bars and then suddenly feels aligned on the seventeenth. Nothing in the actual tempo has changed; the effect comes from a subtle combination of engine vibration, hand timing, and the boat’s movement against the river.

She builds the arrangement around that perceptual shift. The first section contains a dry kora ostinato, cross-stick, and one fretless bass pedal. At bar seventeen, a displaced kick enters half a subdivision later than expected, making the entire groove seem to settle without any obvious downbeat correction. Lena answers with a two-note bassoon figure that repeats every five bars, delaying full alignment even further.

The track never resolves through a large chorus. Instead, the final section gradually removes the ferry recording until the musicians are left with the groove they learned from it. The point is not that the environment was magical; it is that the players discovered a timing relationship they could reproduce after the original sound was gone.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Pre-dawn river ferry moving against a dark current, kora strings in sharp foreground focus, handwritten “17” on a small paper timing note, fretless bass resting against a bench, bassoon bell beside a stainless rail, mist and faint engine vibration suggested through subtle motion blur. Spoken fragments limited to “not yet,” “seventeen,” “now it sits,” and “keep the hand where it was.”

Track 2: Ticket Punches in Three Directions
- Story & Context: Mara records an old mechanical ticket punch at three ferry terminals and discovers that each unit has a slightly different metallic click and spring return. Rather than sampling them as novelty percussion, she assigns one terminal to each layer of a broken-beat pattern: low click for ghost kick support, sharp click for displaced snare, and spring return for offbeat texture.

Amadou writes a kora response figure whose accents rotate every three repetitions. Kojo answers with muted fretless notes that slide upward only a semitone, barely enough to sound melodic but enough to disturb the tonal center. Lena stays out for the first half, then enters with short dry bassoon notes placed exactly where the ticket-punch pattern leaves space.

Near the end, Mara removes all three ticket samples and leaves the drum pattern intact. The electronic groove now carries the shape of the field recordings without reproducing them, demonstrating how environmental rhythm can become composition rather than decoration.
