Track 3: The River Keeps the Late Snare
- Story & Context: At a riverside rehearsal, the group argues over whether one snare should be moved closer to the grid. Kojo insists it is late enough to feel unstable; Mara argues that moving it forward destroys the breathing room around Amadou’s kora figure. They record both versions.

Lena listens back and notices that the later snare leaves enough space for one low bassoon grace note that disappears in the corrected version. The band chooses the late take and builds the entire arrangement around that small pocket. Kojo plays a sliding bass phrase that lands just after the snare, while Amadou keeps the kora pattern almost unchanged for the whole track.

The climax arrives quietly: drums thin to kick, late snare, and shaker, while bassoon and fretless bass cross in opposite directions beneath the repeating kora. A cassette-printed duplicate of the kora drifts slightly flat for eight bars, then cuts out cleanly. The groove remains imperfect but internally balanced.
